LeetCode 1425 — Constrained Subsequence Sum
From Brute Force → Most Optimal
All approaches + code + explanation + dry run
1. Problem Understanding

Given:

nums[]
k

Choose a subsequence such that for every two consecutive selected elements:

index difference <= k

We need to maximize the sum.

Example
nums = [10, 2, -10, 5, 20]
k = 2

One optimal subsequence is:

10 → 2 → 5 → 20

Sum:

10 + 2 + 5 + 20 = 37

Answer:

37
Key DP Observation

Define:

dp[i] = maximum sum of a valid subsequence ending at i

At index i, we have two choices:

Start a new subsequence
dp[i] = nums[i]
Extend a previous subsequence

We can come from any index:

i-k ... i-1

So:

dp[i] = nums[i] + max(0, dp[i-k ... i-1])

Therefore:

dp[i] = nums[i] + max(0, maximum dp in previous k positions)

The entire problem becomes:

Efficiently find the maximum DP value in a sliding window of size k.

Approach 1 — Brute Force Recursion
Idea

At every index, try all possible previous indices within distance k.

For every element:

dp[i] = nums[i]


for j = i-1 ... i-k:
    dp[i] = max(dp[i], nums[i] + dp[j])

This directly follows the recurrence.

Code
class Solution {
    public int constrainedSubsetSum(int[] nums, int k) {


        int n = nums.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n];


        int answer = nums[0];


        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {


            dp[i] = nums[i];


            for (int j = Math.max(0, i - k); j < i; j++) {


                dp[i] = Math.max(
                    dp[i],
                    nums[i] + dp[j]
                );
            }


            answer = Math.max(answer, dp[i]);
        }


        return answer;
    }
}

Although this is technically a DP solution, it is the straightforward/brute-force DP because we scan all k previous states.

Complexity

For every index, we may check k previous elements.

Time  = O(n × k)
Space = O(n)

If:

n = 10^5
k = 10^5

this can become too slow.

Approach 2 — DP + Priority Queue / Max Heap

The problem with Approach 1 is:

max(dp[i-k ... i-1])

takes O(k) if we scan the window.

Instead, maintain the maximum DP value using a max heap.

The heap stores:

(dp value, index)

At index i:

Remove elements outside the window.
Take the maximum DP from the heap.
Calculate dp[i].
Insert the new DP value.
Code
class Solution {
    public int constrainedSubsetSum(int[] nums, int k) {


        int n = nums.length;


        int[] dp = new int[n];


        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq =
            new PriorityQueue<>(
                (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b[0], a[0])
            );


        int answer = Integer.MIN_VALUE;


        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {


            // Remove indices outside the window
            while (!pq.isEmpty() &&
                   pq.peek()[1] < i - k) {


                pq.poll();
            }


            // Best previous dp
            if (!pq.isEmpty() && pq.peek()[0] > 0) {
                dp[i] = nums[i] + pq.peek()[0];
            } else {
                dp[i] = nums[i];
            }


            answer = Math.max(answer, dp[i]);


            pq.offer(new int[]{dp[i], i});
        }


        return answer;
    }
}

Complexity

Each element enters and leaves the heap once.

Heap operation:

O(log n)

Therefore:

Time  = O(n log n)
Space = O(n)

Better than O(nk), but we can do even better.

Approach 3 — DP + Monotonic Deque ⭐

This is the optimal approach.

We need:

maximum dp value

inside the last k positions.

Instead of a heap, maintain a monotonic decreasing deque.

The deque contains DP values in decreasing order:

largest
   ↓
[20, 15, 10, 5]

Therefore:

deque.front()

is always the maximum DP value in the valid window.

Why Can We Remove Smaller Values?

Suppose the deque contains:

[20, 15, 10]

and we calculate:

dp[i] = 25

Then:

[20, 15, 10]

are all smaller than 25.

Since 25 is newer and larger, those smaller values can never become the maximum before 25 expires.

So remove them:

[20, 15, 10]
        ↓
[25]

This is the monotonic deque trick.

Your Code

Your solution uses an especially clever optimization: it stores the DP values directly back into A.

class Solution {
    public int constrainedSubsetSum(int[] A, int k) {


        int res = A[0];


        Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();


        for (int i = 0; i < A.length; ++i) {


            A[i] += !q.isEmpty() ? q.peek() : 0;


            res = Math.max(res, A[i]);


            while (!q.isEmpty() && A[i] > q.peekLast()) {
                q.pollLast();
            }


            if (A[i] > 0) {
                q.offer(A[i]);
            }


            if (i >= k &&
                !q.isEmpty() &&
                q.peek() == A[i - k]) {


                q.poll();
            }
        }


        return res;
    }
}

Understanding Your Code

There are four important operations.

1. Add the best previous DP
A[i] += !q.isEmpty() ? q.peek() : 0;

Because:

q.peek()

is the maximum DP value among the previous valid positions.

Therefore:

A[i] = A[i] + max(previous DP)

If the deque is empty:

A[i] = A[i]

This also automatically handles starting a new subsequence.

2. Update answer
res = Math.max(res, A[i]);

We don't necessarily have to end at the final index.

So every A[i] could potentially be the answer.

3. Maintain decreasing deque
while (!q.isEmpty() && A[i] > q.peekLast())
    q.pollLast();

Suppose:

Deque:
[20, 15, 10]

and:

A[i] = 25

Then:

25 > 10 → remove 10
25 > 15 → remove 15
25 > 20 → remove 20

Deque becomes:

[25]

Thus the deque remains monotonically decreasing.

4. Keep only positive DP values
if (A[i] > 0)
    q.offer(A[i]);

Why?

The recurrence is:

dp[i] = nums[i] + max(0, previous dp)

If the best previous DP is negative, using it would make the sum worse.

Therefore we simply start a new subsequence.

Example:

nums[i] = 5
best previous dp = -10

We don't want:

5 + (-10) = -5

We choose:

5

That's why negative DP values don't need to enter the deque.

5. Remove Expired Elements
if (i >= k &&
    !q.isEmpty() &&
    q.peek() == A[i - k])
    q.poll();

The valid previous indices for i are:

[i-k, i-1]

When we move to the next index, an old value can leave the window.

For example:

i = 5
k = 3

Valid indices:

2, 3, 4

Index:

5 - 3 = 2

will eventually expire.

