Why the Deque Gives O(n)

Every DP value:

1. enters the deque at most once
2. is removed from the back at most once
3. is removed from the front at most once

Therefore total deque operations are linear.

O(n) + O(n) + O(n)
= O(n)

Approach 4 — DP + Segment Tree

Another possible solution is to maintain range maximum queries using a segment tree.

For each i, query:

max(dp[i-k ... i-1])

Then:

dp[i] = nums[i] + max(0, rangeMaximum)
Code
class Solution {


    int[] tree;
    int n;


    void update(int node, int left, int right,
                int index, int value) {


        if (left == right) {
            tree[node] = value;
            return;
        }


        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;


        if (index <= mid) {
            update(node * 2, left, mid, index, value);
        } else {
            update(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, right, index, value);
        }


        tree[node] = Math.max(
            tree[node * 2],
            tree[node * 2 + 1]
        );
    }


    int query(int node, int left, int right,
              int ql, int qr) {


        if (ql > right || qr < left) {
            return Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        }


        if (ql <= left && right <= qr) {
            return tree[node];
        }


        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;


        return Math.max(
            query(node * 2, left, mid, ql, qr),
            query(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, right, ql, qr)
        );
    }


    public int constrainedSubsetSum(int[] nums, int k) {


        n = nums.length;
        tree = new int[4 * n];


        int[] dp = new int[n];


        int answer = Integer.MIN_VALUE;


        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {


            int best = Integer.MIN_VALUE;


            if (i > 0) {
                int left = Math.max(0, i - k);
                int right = i - 1;


                best = query(
                    1, 0, n - 1,
                    left, right
                );
            }


            dp[i] = nums[i];


            if (best > 0) {
                dp[i] += best;
            }


            update(
                1, 0, n - 1,
                i, dp[i]
            );


            answer = Math.max(answer, dp[i]);
        }


        return answer;
    }
}

Complexity

Range maximum:

O(log n)

Update:

O(log n)

For n elements:

Time  = O(n log n)
Space = O(n)

It works, but the monotonic deque is simpler and faster.

Complete Comparison
Approach	Idea	Time	Space
1. Brute Force DP	Check previous k states	O(nk)	O(n)
2. Max Heap	Sliding-window maximum with heap	O(n log n)	O(n)
3. Segment Tree	Range maximum query	O(n log n)	O(n)
4. Monotonic Deque ⭐	Maintain maximum of last k DP values	O(n)	O(n)
⭐ Most Optimal Solution

Your code is the best approach:

class Solution {
    public int constrainedSubsetSum(int[] A, int k) {


        int res = A[0];


        Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();


        for (int i = 0; i < A.length; ++i) {


            // Add the best previous DP value
            A[i] += !q.isEmpty() ? q.peek() : 0;


            // Update global maximum
            res = Math.max(res, A[i]);


            // Maintain decreasing deque
            while (!q.isEmpty() && A[i] > q.peekLast()) {
                q.pollLast();
            }


            // Only positive DP values are useful
            if (A[i] > 0) {
                q.offer(A[i]);
            }


            // Remove expired value
            if (i >= k &&
                !q.isEmpty() &&
                q.peek() == A[i - k]) {


                q.poll();
            }
        }


        return res;
    }
}

⚠️ One Important Point About This Code

This solution modifies the input array A.

For example:

A = [10, 2, -10, 5, 20]

eventually becomes:

A = [10, 12, 2, 17, 37]

That's intentional: the array is being reused as the dp array.

If you don't want to modify the input, use a separate dp[] array.

